<section class="mp-market-explainer" aria-labelledby="under-explainer-heading">
    <h2 id="under-explainer-heading">How Under 2.5 Goals picks are built</h2>
    <p>
        Under 2.5 Goals is the calm side of the totals market: the published view is that the match
        is more likely to finish with two goals or fewer. Unders often show up in tighter leagues,
        weather-affected pitches, or fixtures where both attacks look blunt relative to the defences
        they face. They can unravel quickly when an early goal forces a chase.
    </p>

    <h3>What this card is for</h3>
    <p>
        The Under page lists only fixtures that cleared the live Under 2.5 publish threshold after
        calibration. You can filter by league, kickoff window, live/finished status, and confidence
        band. Each row is meant to be scannable: who is playing, when they kick off, how strong the
        under angle is, and — once settled — whether the total stayed at two or below.
    </p>

    <h3>How Under relates to Over</h3>
    <p>
        Inside the pipeline, Under 2.5 is the complement of Over 2.5 after the ensemble blend is
        renormalised. That keeps the two-way market mathematically consistent: the model is not free
        to claim 80% Over and 80% Under on the same fixture. Bookmaker Under prices (margin removed),
        Dixon-Coles expected goals, Elo form, and the third-party feed (when available) all inform
        the blend before correction and calibration.
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>Publish only if calibrated Under probability clears its own threshold.</li>
        <li>Settlement uses full-time total goals; stoppage-time strikes still count.</li>
        <li>Live unders that go 3+ mid-match are already failed even before full time.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Interpreting confidence</h3>
    <p>
        High Under confidence means the model sees a compressed scoring environment, not that the
        match will be goalless. A 1–1 or 2–0 still lands the under; a 2–1 does not. Compare confidence
        across today’s published unders, then cross-check recent settled performance on
        <a href="/results">Results</a>. The longer technical story lives on
        <a href="/methodology">Methodology</a>.
    </p>

    <h3>Why the list can be short</h3>
    <p>
        Attack-heavy rounds, derbies, or high-tempo leagues may produce few unders that clear the
        gate. Mid-season fixture congestion can cut both ways. An empty or thin Under card usually
        means the threshold did its job. Selection lists that favour lower-scoring legs may still
        appear later on <a href="/betslips">Betslips</a>.
    </p>

    <h3>Responsible use</h3>
    <p>
        One late goal ruins an under. Treat published unders as statistical estimates for adults
        (18+). MatchPredictor does not accept stakes. Read
        <a href="/Privacy#responsible-use">responsible use</a> and the <a href="/faq">FAQ</a>.
    </p>
</section>
